{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And a man went from the house of Levi.</b> Our Sages said that he went following his daughter's counsel (Sotah 12a). And R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Exodus 2:1) that in many cities the Israelites were dwelling, and this woman was in another city — and what need is there for Scripture to mention this? In my opinion, it is because he did not fear Pharaoh's decree and took a wife to beget children from her, that Scripture speaks thus. For whenever one girds himself to do a new thing, Scripture speaks thus: \"and Reuben went and lay with Bilhah\" (Genesis 35:22); \"and he went and took Gomer the daughter of Diblaim\" (Hosea 1:3); \"come and let us sell him to the Ishmaelites\" (Genesis 37:27); \"come and let us smite him with the tongue\" (Jeremiah 18:18); \"come now and let us reason together\" (Isaiah 1:18). So here too he girded himself, <b>and he took the daughter of Levi.</b> And Scripture did not mention the name of the man nor the name of the wife he took, and this was because it would be necessary to trace their lineage and mention their names — who their father was and the father of their father, back to Levi — and now He wished to abbreviate down to the birth of the deliverer, and afterwards (below, 6:14–25) He would trace the lineage of the rest of the tribes as well, for his sake, in the second arrangement.<br><b>And according to</b> the plain sense, that this was the beginning of the marriage, there is no earlier and later in the passage, for this was before Pharaoh's decree, and she bore Miriam and Aaron, and afterwards Pharaoh decreed \"every son that is born, into the river you shall cast him,\" and she bore this good son, and Scripture did not mention the birth of Miriam and Aaron because there was nothing novel in their birth. And according to the opinion of our Sages (Sotah 12b), they were a second marriage — that he had separated from her because of Pharaoh's decree, and took her back through the prophecy of his daughter (see there 13a), and performed an act of marriage with her: he seated her in a canopy, with Miriam and Aaron dancing before them, in their joy that through this Israel would be redeemed. And even though Aaron was young, HaShem put joy in his heart concerning this matter, or Miriam his sister taught him."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The phrase הוושיבה באפריון (literally 'he seated her in an apiryon/canopy') refers to the marriage procession described in the Talmud; I render it literally without expanding on the Talmudic context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:01:14.083439835Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}